# Agent loop

`Session` resolves the workspace, owns a `SkillRegistry`, and starts `messages` with `system_prompt` plus `get_menu()`. Nothing is loaded at the start. The registry adds one `mcp` skill per reachable entry in `MCP_SERVERS`. An `http` or `https` URL in that server's `env` that is not accepting connections is omitted. The registry starts each remaining server and runs the handshake when it is built; a handshake that cannot finish removes that skill. `load_skill` on one of those reuses that process, or restarts it when it has died, and only then adds its tool schemas. `unload_skill` removes those schemas and leaves the process running. `reset()` closes MCP processes, allocates a new workspace directory, then rebuilds that registry and system message, keeping the model, `project`, `coding`, `debug`, and the cached context limit, and clearing `context_tokens`. `resume()` loads `transcript.json` from an existing workspace, restores `model`, `messages`, `project`, and `coding`, closes the previous MCP processes, replays `load_skill` and `unload_skill`, then loads any skill named in the transcript's `skills` list, then `sync_prompt()`. Process exit closes MCP processes too. A missing or invalid project directory fails resume. A transcript with no `project` clears the launch grant. A transcript with no `coding`, or with `coding` false, clears a launch coding session. A non-boolean `coding` value fails resume. `run` takes an injected client; production uses the lazy `get_client()` singleton. Each completion receives `session.skills.schemas()`, not the full `TOOLS` list. The main completion requests `stream=True` and `stream_options` with `include_usage`. A response that already has `choices[0].message` is still accepted. Streamed deltas are joined into one message and stored through `assistant_message`; `reasoning` and `reasoning_content` deltas share one field, and tool-call fragments join by index. `session.on_delta(kind, text)` receives non-empty `reasoning` and `content` pieces as they arrive. When `session.debug` is set, `session.on_debug(system, request)` runs before each main completion. `system` is the prompt on the first completion and again only when that text changes; otherwise it is `None`. `reset()` and `resume()` show the prompt again on the next completion. `request` is the other messages on that call. The summary call does not use that hook. `debug` is a launch flag, kept by `reset()` and `resume()`, and is not stored in the transcript. A connection or timeout error discards the partial message and keeps the existing failure text. An abort inside the tool loop appends `Error: interrupted before this tool ran` as the result of every tool call not yet answered, and logs each one, so the stored assistant turn is never left with unanswered `tool_calls`. OpenAI calls go through `create_completion`, which sends `max_tokens` from config on every call, including the summary. If the server rejects `stream_options`, that completion is retried without it; if it rejects `max_tokens`, the limit is resent as `max_completion_tokens`. Each retry removes a key, so a call makes at most three attempts. The summary call stays non-streaming. After `load_skill` or `unload_skill`, refresh `messages[0]` so the menu marks the active skills.

Store model turns only through `assistant_message`: `role`, `content`, and `tool_calls` of `{id, type, function: {name, arguments}}`. When the model sent non-empty `reasoning` or `reasoning_content`, store it as `reasoning` and send that field back on the next completion. Omit `reasoning` when it is missing or empty. When Claude returns thinking or redacted thinking blocks, store them as `thinking_blocks` and replay those blocks, signatures included, on the next Messages request. OpenAI completions omit `thinking_blocks`. Drop SDK extras such as `index`. Append tool results as `{"role": "tool", "tool_call_id", "content"}`.

When `provider` is `anthropic`, completions and the summary call go through `anthropic_api.py`. The system message is a top-level `system` string, tool schemas become `{name, description, input_schema}`, and tool results travel as `tool_result` blocks on a user message. Consecutive same-role messages are merged. `max_tokens` is sent on every call. Stream events map `thinking_delta` to `reasoning` and `text_delta` to `content`. `context_tokens` stores `input_tokens + output_tokens`. Connection and timeout errors use the same sentences as the OpenAI path, and a partial stream is discarded the same way.

A turn with no tool calls returns `content` when it is non-empty, otherwise `reasoning`, otherwise `The model returned an empty answer.`

Log one JSON object per line to `session.log` inside the workspace: `turn`, `model` (pass the stored message through `_log_preview` so long `reasoning` is truncated at 500 chars), `tool_call` (truncate arg previews past 500 chars), `tool_result`, and `compact` when older turns are summarized. When `MAX_TURNS` is exhausted, append and return the assistant message `Max turns reached.` and log that model event. When a completion cannot reach the API, append and return `Could not reach the API.` A timed-out completion returns `The API request timed out.` A status error such as a 400 returns `The API rejected the request: ` plus the API's message, with the nested `error.message` when the body has one. Either way, log that model event and leave the history otherwise unchanged. `list_models` exits with the same sentence.

Before each completion, and again after a turn finishes, if `context_tokens` is at least `COMPACT_AT` of `context_limit`, summarize older messages into one `Context compacted:` note. `/compact` forces that summary even when usage is under the threshold. Keep the system prompt, the current user message, a tool result the model has not answered yet, and a finished answer. Earlier tool rounds in the current turn are compacted too. The summary call sends no tools. A failed or empty summary leaves the history unchanged. A middle that is empty or only a compaction note is left alone. After a compaction, `context_tokens` is the note's `context_after` estimate, not the summary call's usage, until the next completion reports usage.

`run` also writes `transcript.json` (`model`, the full `messages`, including untruncated reasoning, `context_tokens` when a completion reported `usage.total_tokens`, `skills` when any skill is loaded, `project` when a folder is granted, and `coding` when the session is a coding session) with an atomic replace of a dotfile temp. `resume()` restores that count and clears the cached context limit. `list_sessions()` returns workspaces under `BASE_DIR/workspaces` (`~/.harnh3x/workspaces` by default) whose transcript has a user message, newest first. Unreadable files and `session.log`-only directories are skipped.

Prompt contract: the prompt states the date, the OS, a one-line workspace listing, and the saved memory keys. Call `load_skill` before using a capability; several skills can stay loaded, and `unload_skill` is for skills that will not be needed again. A skill name is not a tool. The menu says so, and a loaded skill lists its tool names. Calling a skill name as a tool returns an error that names that skill's tools and, when the skill is already loaded, says so. A second load_skill for a loaded skill repeats those tool names. Call those tools through the tool interface. Report tool errors. Answer from sources that were read, not from search snippets. Use the `tasks` skill for multi-step jobs. When the user asks for a program, script, or code change, load the `coding` skill and write a workspace file, or a project file when a project is granted; use `python` only to compute an answer. A coding session replaces that contract: read the project before changing it, load the `coding` skill before reading or changing source (it lists, searches, edits, and runs commands), make the smallest change, and confirm the paths. `load_skill` returns the skill body only; the sandbox, yolo, and project rules ride on each tool's schema description through `tools/scope.py`. Cursor rules load only in a coding session. `sync_prompt` puts always-apply rule text in the system prompt and lists other rules by description and globs. After a file tool whose `path`, `source`, or `destination` resolves inside the project, each matching glob rule is appended to that tool result once per process. A project grant without `coding` does not load those rules. Always confirm what was done. Per-skill conventions live in that skill's `SKILL.md` and come back in the `load_skill` result.

```python
# BAD: forwarding the raw SDK message
session.messages.append(msg.model_dump())

# GOOD: the stored shape tests assert
stored = {
    "role": "assistant",
    "content": msg.content,
    "reasoning": msg.reasoning,  # only when the model sent non-empty thinking
    "tool_calls": [{
        "id": tc.id,
        "type": "function",
        "function": {"name": tc.function.name, "arguments": tc.function.arguments},
    }],
}
```
